stuck.
Ron Howard was given the book by his father , and he told me he used it on
every ő his films É with Apollo 13.  Other experienced writers,
including a number of award winners, mentioned they had read the book
and found it very useful Íir work.
I noticed how often people told me they used  the book. Clearly they weren’ t
just reading it, but trying to apply its concepts ả them make better
scripts. As several people told me, “It can make a bad script passable and a
good script great!”
As I started ě the third edition, I wanted above all Í book to be
practical for writers, whether new or experienced, whether in college and
university classes or using it in screenwriting groups.
As I rethought the book, I decided to take out the mythology chapter that
was in ä two editions. When I first wrote that chapter , there were no
books º market £ area of myth and screenwriting (although there
were seminars º topic). Now there are several excellent books on
mythology by people who have carved their niche in this area and studied it
far more than I have. Chris V ogler ’ s book The W riter ’ s Journey  came out
shortly after mine. Pamela Jaye Smith  now has three books º subject—
Inner Drives , The Power ¡ Dark Side , and Beyond the Her o’ s Journey .
Sarah Beach wrote The Scribbler ’ s Guide ¦ Land of Myth: Mythic
Motifs for Storytellers,  and James Bonnet wrote Stealing Fir e fr om the
Gods . The mythology chapter from Making a Good Script Gr eat  has been
reprinted £ textbook Signs of Life £ USA , edited by Sonin Maask
and Jack Solomon.
Fț present edition of Making a Good Script Gr eat , I updated my
examples to newer films, except for several classics ý Some Like It
Hot  and The Sting.  As I picked new examples, I usually tried to choose
films that both did well ¹ box of fice and achieved some sort of critical
acclaim. I’ve also occasionally cited films that are not particularly great
films but provide good examples of some aspect of screenwriting. Don’ t
presume that because I mention á £ book I think it’ s perfect. There
are few perfect films; even Academy A ward winners often have flaws.
Although most of my examples come from films that I think are very good,
if not necessarily terrific, I also believe we can learn from many «
types of films, and even a mediocre one sometimes does something well.
This edition also contains three new chapters: discussions of ş view
and of creating cinematic images and ², and a new case study of
screenwriter Paul Haggis. Throughout the book, I extended my coverage of
some concepts, but if I felt my coverage of a topic £ previous edition
was thorough and said what needed to be said, I often updated the examples
but left the discussion as it was. In all my decisions, I took the position that
“If it ain’ t broke, ¥ fix it!”
I chose to add a case study of ī , Paul Haggis, to this edition. Paul is
ő č successful writers in Hollywood. He has been nominated
for three Academy A wards for Best Screenplay ( Million Dollar Baby ,
Letters fr om Iwo Jima,  and Crash ) and won for Crash.  He has won two
Emmys for his television writing for thirtysomething  and Due South . And
he’ s been nominated for a number of awards in many genres—including for
Üs Casino Royale  and î V alley of Elah .
I hope this book continues to be enjoyable and useful and makes many good
