Intr oduction
S creenwriting, like any other art form, is both an art and a craft. W riters
express themselves đ stories that drive them, ās they
Ú bring to life, the idećy Ú convey , © styles they
choose to express their personalities, values, and individual takes on life.
Learning ě includes learning to speak in a personal voice. Y ou
become an artist by putting yourself into your work. Y our work becomes
compelling and deep because ¡ personal and artistic odyssey you take
£ writing process.
But it ° enough to ð express oneself. Screenwriting is also a craft.
A screenplay’ s form is vastly « from a novel’ s or a short ß. One
has to know ż convey the elements of drama in a way that serves the
particular mediums of ´ writing—film, television, theater , and W eb.
It ° enough ě well. One has ě well for a visual medium. T o
become not just ō artist, but great ¹ craft  of screenwriting, a
whole new set of skills õ be learned. These skills include knowing
ż tell µ , Ã É, middle, and end, that is clear upº
first viewing and À told within a specified time limit. This might mean
telling µ in a few minutes for a short film, in twenty-two to thirty
minutes for a sitcom, in forty-two to sixty minutes for a television series, or
in ninety minutes to two hours for most films.
Screenwriters ¼ learn ż integrate their themes and ideas into
stories without getting preachy or giving a lecture Ç meaning of life.
Screenwriters ¼ learn ż use the cinema—ż tell µ
through action, ¯, and images.
The screenwriter has to learn to reveal characters succinctly through a
ß twists and turns, đ telling gesture, đ small
character details that show us what makes ¬ tick.
These are ą the craft. They À learned, and when integrated with
the art of screenwriting, they can lead ¦ making of ō film.
Making ō script ó just a matter of having ă idea. In
screenwriting it’ s the art © craft, the writing © rewriting, that
make ă script great.
Art without craft is ð self-expression. Craft without art is predictable
and by-the-book. But the two together? That’ s where great films come
from.
*****
If you’re writing your first script, this book will help develop your skills at
telling a compelling and ´ story . If you’re a veteran screenwriter ,
this book will articulate the skills you know intuitively and, hopefully , give
you some additional skills you hadn’ t thought about. And if you are
currently stuck on a rewrite, this book will help you analyze and solve your
script’ s problems and get it back on track.
This book will take you đ complete screenwriting process, from
ä spark of an idea through a full script’ s rewriting. Y ou will learn
ż or ganize your ideas, ż create a compelling · and ż
make your characters dimensional and worthy of our company for two
hours. Y ou’ll also learn ż rewrite your script when you’re finished.
Because if you write, you will rewrite— ê the nature ¡ business.
Unless you’re ī who writes purely for himself and stashes his scripts
in a box £ garage, you will find yourself rewriting your scripts—again
and again. First, you will do it Ñ your first draft “just right.” Then your
friends will å few suggestions, and you’ll rewrite “just ò it a
little better .” Y our agent will have some suggestions about how ò it
more marketable, and you’ll incorporate those. The producer and
development executive will want you to do another rewrite so Áy can
put their own stamp on it. And, once your script is in production, the actors
will have ideas about “what works Ím” and want you ū them
“just a little bit more.”
At every stage ¡ process of rewriting, input from others will influence
your choices. As it should. Screenwriting is ultimately a collaborative
process, combining solitary hours of self-expression with working in
tandem with other artists to realize a vision through a visual medium.
This is all well and good, provided you know what to rewrite and provided
that every rewrite improves í. Unfortunately , this rarely happens.
Many people feel like the man who decided to become ī because he
saw so much bad writing on television and in film. He declared, “Certainly
I can do better than that!” After submitting his script to a producer who
turned him down, he protested, “But it’ s much better than anything else I’ve
seen!” “Of course,” said the producer . “Anyone can write better than that.
The trick is ě so brilliantly that after everyone ruins it in rewrites, it’ s
still watchable.”
And it’ s true. Many scripts get worse and worse £ rewrite process. The
further ì gets from its inspirational source, the more muddled it
becomes. It ñ to lose its magic. By the fifth rewrite, certain elements
no longer make sense. By the twelfth rewrite, ¤ is completely
«, and no one wants to do Ü anymore.
The solution might seem to be “Don’ t rewrite!” Unfortunately , that ó an
alternative. Most first-draft scripts, regardless of Đ creativity and magic
Áir writers put Óm, ¥ work. Many are overwritten—they’re
ð too long ò ă, salable film. î heat ¡ creative
moment, many writers lose sight of essentials—paying of f a clue,
completing a ¨ transformational arc, finishing of f a subplot. A
writer may å germ of an idea running đ script that õ
be strengthened. Rewrites will clarify Đse elements and repair those
that are ailing.
Is there a particular trick to rewriting? Y es! Only rewrite what ª work,
and leave the rest alone. This often means working against the temptation to
do more and more. It means not getting carried away by new and «
ideas that are exciting but ¥ fix your script’ s problems. It means
following suggestions that are designed Ñ í on track, not of f. It
means holding back on a new creative stamp because Ö ’ s original
creative stamp is “just fine, thank you.” If it ain’ t broke, ¥ fix it! And if
it is broke, do something!
So how is this done? This book is designed to discuss just that. It’ s designed
to discuss what “on track” means, and to look at concepts that À
applied è É ¡ writing process đ rewrites to
help make ă script better . It’ s designed to teach you how ě and
rewrite ef fectively , without losing the magic needed £ important last
draft—the shooting script.
My career as ì consultant has focused almost exclusively on ż
get í on track so that problems are solved © original creativity
is preserved. I’ve worked on several thousand scripts for feature films, as
well as television scripts and plays. I’ve worked on miniseries, television
movies, ´ series, and sitcoms, and on horror films, action-
adventures, sci-fi, mystery , comedies, dramas, and fantasies. Whether I
work with writers, producers, directors, or executives, the challenge is
always ø: How can we make the next draft work? T ogether , our job
is to define í’ s concepts, analyze its problems, and find the
solutions to those problems that will create a workable draft. I’ve worked
with many of č creative and successful people £ business and
realized that everyone, no matter how experienced, suf fers from problems
in writing and rewriting.
Rewriting Îies usually occur because í’ s problems aren’ t
well defined or analyzed before starting the rewrite. The producer might
say , vaguely , “It’ s ı act,” so a rewrite is done to fix ı act.
Then the director says, “Now I see we å problem ¾ main
ç” so another rewrite focuses º ö . Next, another
rewrite is undertaken to fix the subplot. But the subplot rewrite throws the
main plot of f track, so another rewrite tries to fix that.
Since í works as a whole, changes in one ą it may require
changes in another . My job as ì consultant is to identify and analyze
í’ s problems befor e  the rewrite and to work ¾ creators to
make sure that Đ problems get solved, without introducing even more
problems. I’ve discovered Á process of rewriting ì ó just an
amorphous, magical, “maybe-it-works, maybe-it-ª” kind of process.
There are specific elements that make ă script great, elements that can
be consciously analyzed and improved.
Naturally , since every script is unique, every script’ s problems are «.
It’ s not possible ě a book that you can follow point by point to create
the perfect script. The creative process ó a paint-by-the-numbers
process, and this book ó designed ū you some simple rules and
formulas to apply by rote.
This book ł concepts and principles, not about rules and formulas.
It’ s about what works and why it works. It’ s about appreciation ¡ art of
film © techniques used to express this art. It’ s also about problem
solving. It ł combining intuition and creativity and one’ s instincts
with certain concepts and principles ò sure ì ó just fresh
and original, but that it works .
In my many years of experience, I’ve seen ø kinds of script
problems occur again and again. Problems in exposition. Problems with
structure, with shaping ¤ . Problems with momentum. Problems with
an insuf ficiently developed idea. These are all problems that can make the
dif ference between a sale and another rejection letter , between commercial
success and box-of fice failure.
T o ô how these problems have been solved in great scripts, I’ll
discuss some of č satisfying films throughout a long period of film
history . I’ve chosen films that are good teaching films. They’re well
structured with strong dimensional characters and a well worked-out idea.
But they are not formulás. They’re creative, artistic, and well crafted.
If you haven’ t seen these films, you can rent them and study them, again
and again. Many of ís are now found º Internet. And, of
course, while discovering why they work and how they work, you will also
