MOMENTUM PROBLEMS
Lack of momentum is ő č common problems in films.
Momentum problems usually occur because × not a clear three-act
structure with clear ®s ș ¤ moving. Sometimes
they occur because certain scenes take ¤ of f on a tangent.
Often, momentum problems are misdiagnosed. As a result, the proposed
remedies fail to solve the problems.
Most filmmakers try to fix momentum problems by adding more action. It’ s
not unusual for a television detective series to add a car chase, a fistfight, or
a shootout every time ¤ seems to slow down. But, these actions
rarely solve the problem. Often, they interrupt ¤’ s structure, thereby
slowing its momentum even more once the added action is finished.
Sometimes, filmmakers confuse momentum with pacing and think that if
they up the pace they will mend a momentum problem. But momentum is
the result ¡ relationship of one scene ¦ next, creating action and
reaction. It ª matter whether scenes unfold or move slowly or quickly ,
as long ćy seem connected and keep ¤ moving forward.
Sometimes §s say they think that certain European films work well,
even though they ¥ have momentum. They may be confusing
momentum and pacing. Such films might have momentum yet just move at
a slower pace. This is often true for American films ń. Signs,  although
about great threats and dangers and mysteries and tragedy , was not a fast-
paced film. Much of W itness  was slowly paced (true to its setting on an
Amish farm) but still kept its momentum going.
Sometimes, to solve a momentum problem, writers pick up a ß pace,
sometimes hurrying the plot ¦ extent Áre’ s no letup Í
§. If a movie’ s pace is too fast, ± ë catch up with its
story . Therefore, instead of being “with” ¿ ± feels as if
¤ is always ahead ¡m, unattainable. This can happen ¾
relentless action in some action-adventures.
When §s lose interest in µ , the problem may have little to do
with story movement. If a ơ too predictable, §s will lose
interest. If there are no subplots to dimensionalize ¿ or if the
characters are stereotypes who fail to involve us, § interest will
